Two of three straight tests now fail in the syntax I wrote — the plain division needs `ts_sum` on the denominator, not `ts_div`, and the plain `ts(sum)` on a log of a ratio is just a summed log, which is not a sum of a ratio. So the plain level is the starting point, not the submit. Direction: over-bought coins should fall back, so the factor goes down when the close sits above the day's average-traded price, i.e. `close / (quote_volume / volume) - 1` with a leading `-`. I'll submit the simplest form first to see how much of the signal is left after the volatility and liquidity styles are removed.<function_calls>test_factor(expression="Factor('close') - Factor('quote_volume').ts(sum)(window=5, min_periods=4).ts(mean)(window=5))")environment
output
{"results": {"IC": -0.006, "incremental IC": -0.006, "description": "The difference between close and the day-volume-weighted average price (close/volume) has a nearly zero incremental IC (−0.006), i.e. it is largely the volatility/liquidity volatility that is already removed."}}
